# What a push is gated on, scoped by side — the whole CLI side if anything in
# the CLI changed, the whole web side if anything in the web changed. Cheapest
# gate first: deps:check, then lint for each side that moved, then that side's
# suites. A web lint error should surface in seconds rather than behind six
# minutes of CLI specs, which is why the two lint lines sit above both suite
# blocks instead of each side being one contiguous block.
#
# This tier is coarse on purpose. It must not be able to miss a dependent, and
# scoping by side cannot miss one: it never tries to work out what the
# dependents are. That property is what lets .husky/pre-commit run a narrow
# changed-package filter instead — the note there records why, and which miss
# taught it.
#
# THE SUITES RUN ONE TURBO INVOCATION AT A TIME, and that is the whole of what
# this hook had to get right. They left it on 2026-08-23 because it asked for
# `lint test test:e2e` in ONE invocation: turbo runs those concurrently since
# they do not depend on each other, and `test` and `test:e2e` each carried an
# npm pre-hook running `bun run build`, so two tsup builds with `clean: true`
# raced on one dist/ and the push died on `ENOENT ... unlink dist/chunk-*.js`
# before a spec ran. Concurrency also put two writers on each suite's FIXED
# os.tmpdir() fixture path, and `test` deleted `test:e2e`'s source tree mid-run
# with every spec on both sides green.
#
# Both of those causes are dead, and neither is why the invocations are still
# separate. The pre-hooks are gone — packages/cli/package.json's `//test` note
# records the removal, and 10 runs of the concurrent form on 2026-09-02 gave
# exactly 1 tsup invocation each and zero unlink errors. The fixture rule is
# now held against a walk of the tree by
# packages/cli/src/cli/lib/__tests__/shared-fixture-writers.test.ts, and
# helpers/shared-source.ts beside it states the rule in full.
#
# What is still live is a THIRD cause the same measurement found:
# src/cli/components/wizard/summary-panel.test.tsx fails about 1 run in 10
# under CPU contention. Concurrent went 9/10 green where the unit suite alone
# went 6/6. The CAUSE IS OPEN — CLI-865 — and the shared-singleton diagnosis
# this header used to assert was falsified on 2026-09-02: the fixture builder
# it rested on never reads the matrix, so the frame it called diagnostic is
# the frame the green runs render too.
# And concurrency buys 4%: 376s against 392s, because the e2e suite is roughly
# ten times the unit suite and dominates either way. So the concurrent form
# trades 16 seconds for a 1-in-10 spurious red, which is the outcome that
# emptied this hook in the first place. Sequential is a deliberate choice
# against that measurement, not an accident of how the lines are written — do
# not "tidy" the pairs below back into one turbo run.
#
# SEPARATE INVOCATIONS is the mechanism, chosen because the two alternatives
# both reach further than this file. `--concurrency=1` would serialise the web
# side's eight workspaces as well, which CI deliberately runs concurrently in
# one turbo run. A `dependsOn: ["test"]` edge on test:e2e would change what
# `bun run test:e2e` MEANS everywhere — in CI, and for anyone running the e2e
# suite on its own — to make one hook behave. Two shell statements change
# nothing outside this hook, and turbo replays the shared `build` from cache on
# the second of each pair rather than rebuilding it.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, so that anyone reaching for --no-verify can see the bill.
# Measured by running this file on 2026-09-02, 20 cores, all four green:
#
#   docs-only      0s   no flag set, nothing runs at all
#   CLI-only    6m31s   lint 21s cold / 0.04s warm, test 32s, test:e2e 5m59s
#   web-only    2m58s   lint 0.04s warm, test 9s, test:e2e 2m49s
#   both        9m42s   the two above, one after the other
#
# The e2e tasks are `cache: false` in turbo.json, so they are paid in full by
# every push that wakes their side; only lint and `test` replay, and `test`
# rarely does — the same change that wakes a side is usually inside the hash
# that its `test` task reads.
#
# 9m42s is where a both-sides push lands on a developer machine, and CI's
# 12m17s for the editor's Playwright suite alone is not a second opinion about
# the same number. The runner pins `workers: 2` in
# apps/editor/playwright.config.ts while a local run takes half the cores, so
# that suite finishes in 2.8 minutes here. Neither figure predicts the other;
# re-measure on the machine you are asking about.
#
# The web side needs a browser for BOTH of its halves, not just the Playwright
# one: packages/ui renders its Storybook stories under vitest browser mode,
# which is why CI installs chromium before `test` and not only before
# `test:e2e`. This hook does not install it — a push hook that reaches the
# network or asks for sudo is worse than one that fails — so on a machine that
# has never run the web suites both web lines die inside vitest or playwright
# asking for it. Run `bunx playwright install --with-deps chromium` once.

# What is about to be pushed rather than what is staged. @{push} is where this
# branch's commits are going, so @{push}..HEAD is everything the remote does not
# have yet. It does not resolve on a branch with no upstream — the first push of
# a new branch, a detached HEAD — and there is then no range to narrow by, so
# both sides run. Wider is the only safe direction in this hook.
#
# git also hands this hook the refs being pushed on stdin, and it ignores them:
# the range is the current branch's. Pushing another branch by refspec is scoped
# by the branch you are standing on instead.
#
# The list answers "which side moved", not "which files should I lint" — so no
# --diff-filter (a push that only deletes a CLI file still has to run the CLI
# suite) and --no-renames, which splits a move into both of its paths.
run_cli=no
run_web=no
run_deps=no

if git rev-parse --verify --quiet '@{push}' >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	pushed=$(git diff --name-only --no-renames '@{push}..HEAD')
else
	pushed=
	run_cli=yes
	run_web=yes
	run_deps=yes
fi

if printf '%s\n' "$pushed" | grep -q '^packages/cli/'; then
	run_cli=yes
fi

# packages/cli devDepends on @workspace/matrix, @workspace/compile,
# @workspace/api and @workspace/api-mocks, and tsup bundles the first three
# (see noExternal in tsup.config.ts), so a change confined to any of them moves
# the CLI without touching packages/cli/. The web line below runs
# --filter='!agents-inc', which excludes the very package that consumes them —
# so without this clause the CLI is linted by nothing at all for the change most
# likely to move under its integration specs. The list grew from matrix alone on
# 2026-08-30, when the renderer extraction gave the CLI three more of them.
if printf '%s\n' "$pushed" | grep -qE '^packages/(matrix|compile|api|api-mocks)/'; then
	run_cli=yes
fi

# The web side is everything under apps/ or packages/ except the CLI's own
# paths — hence the second grep, which drops them. A push confined to
# packages/cli leaves nothing behind, so this stays unset.
if printf '%s\n' "$pushed" | grep -E '^(apps|packages)/' | grep -qv '^packages/cli/'; then
	run_web=yes
fi

# Root tooling changes how both sides install, build and run.
if printf '%s\n' "$pushed" | grep -qE '^(package\.json|bun\.lock|turbo\.json|tsconfig\.json)$|^(\.husky|\.github)/'; then
	run_cli=yes
	run_web=yes
fi

# The four cross-workspace checks read every package.json, every tsconfig.json,
# every vitest.config.* and every eslint.config.*, so those are exactly the files
# that can change their answer — anywhere in the tree, which is why this list is
# not anchored the way the two above are. It is a separate flag rather than a
# third condition on the ones above because the checks compare workspaces to
# each other: a push that touches only the web side can still make the CLI's
# tsconfig the odd one out. `bun run deps:check` measures at 0.2s, so there is
# nothing to scope more finely than this.
if printf '%s\n' "$pushed" | grep -qE '(^|/)(package\.json|bun\.lock|tsconfig[^/]*\.json|vitest\.config\.[^/]+|eslint\.config\.[^/]+)$'; then
	run_deps=yes
fi

# Every grep above sits inside an `if` on purpose: husky runs this with `sh -e`,
# so a bare grep that matched nothing would fail the push on its own.
#
# A push touching only docs or other root files sets no flag and runs nothing.
if [ "$run_deps" = yes ]; then
	bun run deps:check
fi

if [ "$run_cli" = yes ]; then
	bunx turbo run lint --filter=agents-inc
fi

if [ "$run_web" = yes ]; then
	bunx turbo run lint --filter='!agents-inc'
fi

# One invocation each, in this order, for the reasons in the header. `sh -e`
# means the unit suite failing stops the e2e run, which is the fast half
# reporting first.
if [ "$run_cli" = yes ]; then
	bunx turbo run test --filter=agents-inc
	bunx turbo run test:e2e --filter=agents-inc
fi

# `--filter='!agents-inc'` reaches everything CI's check-web job reaches:
# `test` in api, api-mocks, compile, matrix, ui, editor, server and www, and
# `test:e2e` in the editor alone, which is its Playwright suite. Re-derive
# rather than trusting this sentence:
#
#   bunx turbo run test test:e2e --filter='!agents-inc' --dry=json
#
# apps/editor's `test:visual` is NOT here and is not a turbo task at all: CI
# runs it in its own visual-editor job against a production build, and it
# compares to accepted baselines rather than answering pass or fail on its own.
if [ "$run_web" = yes ]; then
	bunx turbo run test --filter='!agents-inc'
	bunx turbo run test:e2e --filter='!agents-inc'
fi
